WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.104
Local development and production have different needs.

2
00:00:03.104 --> 00:00:06.726
Tests often use deterministic data so failures can be reproduced.

3
00:00:06.726 --> 00:00:13.775
Production secrets, tokens, session identifiers, and keys must not depend
on deterministic test seeds or copied sample values.

4
00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:16.814
A test helper that sets a seed is useful in the test suite.

5
00:00:16.814 --> 00:00:21.147
The same pattern used in production token generation is a serious design
error.

6
00:00:21.147 --> 00:00:28.390
Containers, virtual machines, serverless functions, and short-lived
processes at a high level introduce operational questions.

7
00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.977
How does the process obtain system randomness?

8
00:00:30.977 --> 00:00:32.594
What happens during startup?

9
00:00:32.594 --> 00:00:35.763
Are images cloned with generated secrets already inside?

10
00:00:35.763 --> 00:00:40.548
Are deployment scripts creating keys once per environment or once per
container image?

11
00:00:40.548 --> 00:00:43.717
Does concurrency create duplicate generation concerns?

12
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:47.468
These are design and deployment questions, not just code questions.

13
00:00:47.468 --> 00:00:52.189
Load-balanced applications must generate values safely across multiple
instances.

14
00:00:52.189 --> 00:00:57.362
Two servers should not accidentally share a deterministic seed that causes
duplicate tokens.

15
00:00:57.362 --> 00:01:03.506
Environment separation should prevent development, staging, and production
from sharing generated secrets.

16
00:01:03.506 --> 00:01:09.908
Secret generation during deployment should have ownership, auditability,
storage, rotation, and rollback plans.

17
00:01:09.908 --> 00:01:14.629
A generated value becomes an operational asset as soon as another system
depends on it.

18
00:01:14.629 --> 00:01:19.415
Operational ownership matters because generated secrets and tokens have
lifecycles.

19
00:01:19.415 --> 00:01:27.434
Someone should know how values are created, where they are stored, how
long they live, how they are revoked, and how they are rotated after
exposure.

20
00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:38.040
Secure randomness is not only an implementation detail; it is connected to
configuration, deployment, incident response, and the reliability of
authentication and cryptographic workflows.
